WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:08.560
Hey Wisecrack, Helen here.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:13.280
When you spend as much time thinking about
movies as we do, sometimes you wonder if you’re

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.349
just plain overthinking.

00:00:15.349 --> 00:00:18.680
Is You Got Mail a fun-loving rom-com or an
Orwellian masterpiece?

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:21.280
Is Hairspray a metaphor for the Cold War?

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:24.950
And what’s the super secret meaning at the
heart of The Sandlot?

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:29.580
This is all to say -- sometimes when you read
a text too deeply, you go a bit cross-eyed.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:33.730
Which brings us to Zombieland, which we’ve
probably watched dozens of times at this point.

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:37.670
On the surface, the film seems like a pretty
typical mainstream horror-comedy, leaning

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:40.190
more on quippy one-liners than on plot or
theme.

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:42.680
"Time to nut up or shutup"

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:49.179
But after watching Zombieland too many times,
we began to notice some interesting... subtext.

00:00:49.179 --> 00:00:51.999
Could there be hidden depths to the film no
one’s noticed before?

00:00:51.999 --> 00:00:54.690
Is Zombieland far more than just a fun diversion?

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:58.230
Or… have we gone totally off the rails?

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:29.920
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on Zombieland:
Is It Deep or Dumb? and as always, spoilers ahead.

00:01:40.750 --> 00:02:10.300
But first, a quick recap: Zombieland focuses
on a world overtaken by, you guessed it, zombies.

00:02:10.319 --> 00:02:14.550
It’s the typical zom-pocalypse, where pretty
much everyone’s now undead, and the few

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:17.709
living folk struggle to keep themselves from
becoming dinner.

00:02:17.709 --> 00:02:23.370
The film focuses on four outcasts - Columbus
(a nerdy recluse), Tallahassee (a trigger-happy

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:28.790
Woody Harrelson type… played by Woody Harrelson)
and Little Rock &amp; Wichita (two con-artist

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:29.790
sisters).

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:33.930
Together, the group travels west to California,
taking refuge at Bill Murray’s mansion and

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:37.750
then letting off some steam, and a shitton
of bullets, at the local amusement park.

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:42.230
Zombies are decapitated, shot, and smooshed
-- as the dysfunctional quartet works through

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:45.389
their issues and becomes their own make-shift
family.

00:02:45.389 --> 00:02:49.459
Now, the first interesting bit of depth we
noticed about Zombieland is how similar it

00:02:49.459 --> 00:02:50.749
is to a certain genre.

00:02:50.749 --> 00:02:54.459
No, we’re not talking about zombie films
or comedies…

00:02:54.459 --> 00:02:55.769
But the western!

00:02:55.769 --> 00:02:58.230
Just look at John Ford’s quintessential
western, Stagecoach.

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:03.219
In Ford’s film, a group of strangers from
different backgrounds travel West together,

00:03:03.219 --> 00:03:07.799
dealing with their own internal issues while
fending off a different kind of threat: Native

00:03:07.799 --> 00:03:08.799
Americans..

00:03:08.799 --> 00:03:09.799
Sound familiar?

00:03:09.799 --> 00:03:13.749
That’s because it’s more or less the same
basic plot as Zombieland: A group of strangers

00:03:13.749 --> 00:03:17.500
travel West together, dealing with their own
internal issues while fighting off the local

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:21.239
inhabitants - except this time the locals
are the undead.

00:03:21.239 --> 00:03:25.359
Tallahassee is pretty much the textbook gunslinger
in the tradition of a John Wayne or Clint

00:03:25.359 --> 00:03:26.359
Eastwood.

00:03:26.359 --> 00:03:30.459
There’s even a ‘mexican stand-off’ between
Columbus and Tallahassee, complete with Sergio

00:03:30.459 --> 00:03:34.669
Leone esque close-ups of each character’s
eyes and weapons.

00:03:34.669 --> 00:03:38.560
Zombieland uses these Western tropes to draw
a deliberate parallel to our own historical

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.810
expansion further into Native land.

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:44.739
This expansion was justified by a concept
called “manifest destiny,” which held

00:03:44.739 --> 00:03:48.810
that it was Americans' God-given duty and
right to travel west and snatch land away

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:51.079
from the indigineous tribes already living
there

00:03:51.079 --> 00:03:56.930
In Zombieland, four settlers led by a man
named Columbus (ahem), move West, taking everything

00:03:56.930 --> 00:04:00.549
in their path-- cars, guns and the houses
of Beverly Hills..

00:04:00.549 --> 00:04:05.650
And they snatch this land away from the not-quite-people
already there, shooting, stabbing and bashing

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:07.129
all that stands in their way.

00:04:07.129 --> 00:04:12.310
For that matter, the names Tallahassee, Wichita,
and Little Rock are also associated with Native

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:13.310
Americans.

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:17.519
“Tallahassee” is an Apalachee Indian word
for “old town” or “abandoned fields”;

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:21.680
Wichita is “a confederation of Southern
Plains Native American tribes”; and Little

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:26.150
Rock was a place where Native American lived…
but then again I guess they lived everywhere,

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:27.930
so maybe that one doesn’t fit.

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:29.050
Think we’re stretching?

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:32.360
Well, as our motley zombie-killing crew move
further to

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:37.080
California, their destruction crecendoes with
the vandalizing and looting of a Native American

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:38.300
souvenir shop.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:43.199
So is Zombieland actually a sly commentary
on America’s Western Expansion?

00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:48.150
Is the film using its character to critique
our past sins -- eradicating an indigineous

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:51.060
population in the pursuit of some kind of
new ‘Manifest Destiny’?

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:52.730
It’s kind of unclear.

00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:58.870
The film never admonishes the crew’s actions;
on the contrary, it revels in the ruin.

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:03.080
Also is the film really conflating mindless
zombies with Native Americans?

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:08.460
You could interpret this comparison as a commentary
on how colonizers viewed indigenous tribes.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:13.580
Native Americans were often presented as mindless
and uncivilized, a horde intent on just mutilating

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:15.050
&amp; killing.

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:17.919
Not so different from hordes of brain-hungry
zombies

00:05:17.919 --> 00:05:22.270
But if the film were satirizing this mindset…
where’s the satire?.

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:26.240
And as much as we searched for it… there
just isn’t any evidence to prove our theory.

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:30.539
We’re always supposed to side with Columbus
and his friends, never the zombies.

00:05:30.539 --> 00:05:35.389
Shooting and killing these mindless creatures
is the moral &amp; correct solution in Zombieland.

00:05:35.389 --> 00:05:39.720
Compare that to the satirical take on manifest
destiny seen in a Western like Little Big

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:44.990
Man, which depicts the Cheyenne tribe as sympathetic,
the American settlers as pretty dickish, and

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:48.129
life on the American frontier as an absurdist
tragedy.

00:05:48.129 --> 00:05:52.889
In contrast, Zombieland could read like just
a celebration of murdering a ton of quote

00:05:52.889 --> 00:05:57.879
unquote “monsters” to conquer the New
World That also seems like a stretch, one

00:05:57.879 --> 00:05:59.990
that would merit a big “yikes.”

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:04.600
So if this film is deep, it’s a little unclear
if it’s coming from the whole manifest destiny

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:05.600
theme.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:09.919
but there’s a second potential deeper meaning
to Zombieland: As an indictment of our own

00:06:09.919 --> 00:06:12.039
consumer society.

00:06:12.039 --> 00:06:14.770
Zombieland indulges in the destruction of
the social stratum.

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:19.409
In the first image of the film, the American
flag breezes backwards, as the Capitol Building

00:06:19.409 --> 00:06:21.139
burns in the background.

00:06:21.139 --> 00:06:24.150
Symbolizing the destruction of society as
we know it.

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:28.780
Then, in the credits sequence, we watch as
a hodgepodge of regular people (a father with

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:34.860
his child, a fireman, a police officer) are
mercilessly devoured by the zombie hordes.

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:39.070
Zombieland deliberately rejects all the social
shackles that kept people in check.

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:43.509
Transgressional acts, like looting and pillaging,
aren’t just the new normal, they’re laudatory

00:06:43.509 --> 00:06:45.020
acts of defiance.

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:48.689
"Tallahassee firlmy believes that you have
to blow off steam in zombieland or else you

00:06:48.689 --> 00:06:50.539
lose what's left of your mind"

00:06:50.539 --> 00:06:52.810
Even people’s own names are forsaken.

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:55.590
Nobody in Zombieland goes by their given name.

00:06:55.590 --> 00:06:59.910
Instead they take the mantle of their place
of origin, a rejection of old society’s

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:02.259
fancy rules about who gets what name.

00:07:02.259 --> 00:07:04.199
"Wichita, Little Rock"

00:07:04.199 --> 00:07:09.199
The only people who thrive in this new zombie
world are the outcasts of society.

00:07:09.199 --> 00:07:13.449
Columbus was a loner who spent all day at
his computer playing World of Warcraft.

00:07:13.449 --> 00:07:17.909
Wichita &amp; Little Rock were con artists, who
survived by up-selling shitty rings.

00:07:17.909 --> 00:07:23.120
But now that the zombies have taken over and
the world has fallen apart, they set the rules.

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:27.580
Columbus defines the proper ways to survive
in Zombieland, and the film treats these rules

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:28.580
as absolute.

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:33.220
Cardio, seatbelts, and the double tap all
save him and the rest of the gang countless

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:36.060
times, becoming their new pseudo ‘Bill of
Rights.’

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:40.440
Now in charge, Columbus, Tallahassee, Wichita
&amp; Little Rock revel in the destruction of

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:41.840
the commercial past.

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:46.699
When they wreck the Native American gift shop,
it’s framed as a bonding moment amongst

00:07:46.699 --> 00:07:47.699
the quartet.

00:07:47.699 --> 00:07:48.699
"you gotta enjoy the little things"

00:07:48.699 --> 00:07:51.789
Yet despite the chaos, nothing has really
changed.

00:07:51.789 --> 00:07:56.289
Sure, society’s fallen apart but it’s
only ushered in a new social order.

00:07:56.289 --> 00:07:57.289
Columbus and co.

00:07:57.289 --> 00:08:01.870
seek out the same luxuries as their predecessors,
consuming everything in their path, be it

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:04.330
food, cars, mansions or weapons.

00:08:04.330 --> 00:08:08.960
The only difference: now that the rich are
dead and/or zombified, these societal rejects

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:11.050
can just take it for themselves.

00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:15.099
In essence, all they’ve done is become the
upper-echelon by virtue of their survival

00:08:15.099 --> 00:08:16.099
skills.

00:08:16.099 --> 00:08:21.010
So is Zombieland actually about the inevitability
of our social norms or consumer society?Even

00:08:21.010 --> 00:08:26.379
after the world ends, will we still be mindless
consumers beholden to ingrained cultural norms?

00:08:26.379 --> 00:08:31.030
Well, yet again… there’s just not enough
evidence to support this theory.

00:08:31.030 --> 00:08:34.190
Other zombie films have explored similar themes
to far better effect.

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:36.289
Take for instance, Dawn of the Dead.

00:08:36.289 --> 00:08:39.640
A film explicitly about the ills of consumer
culture.

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:44.560
In George Romero’s masterpiece, a few survivors
take refuge in a shopping mall away from the

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:45.560
zombie hords.

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:49.329
There, they senselessly consume everything
in the mall to their hearts’ content.

00:08:49.329 --> 00:08:52.800
That is until the zombies show up to take
the mall back.

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:57.329
In the end credits, the zombies mindlessly
wander around the mall just as our heroes

00:08:57.329 --> 00:09:00.190
did, suggesting there’s no difference between
the two.

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:05.290
If their zombies mindlessly lust for brains,
our survivors mindlessly lust for stuff.

00:09:05.290 --> 00:09:08.589
In Dawn of the Dead there’s no question
about the intent of the film.

00:09:08.589 --> 00:09:11.530
But Zombieland doesn’t have the same critical
edge.

00:09:11.530 --> 00:09:15.300
In the end it seems to tacitly support these
same societal norms.

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:20.350
Through wanton consumerism, our heroes actually
do find convenience &amp; safety with one another.

00:09:20.350 --> 00:09:24.710
The film strays from its more critical points,
opting instead to end on the importance of

00:09:24.710 --> 00:09:30.339
‘family’ above all else, as in: the family
that consumes together, stays together.

00:09:30.339 --> 00:09:31.709
"That face?

00:09:31.709 --> 00:09:37.180
That's me realizing that those smart girls
in that big black truck, and that big guy

00:09:37.180 --> 00:09:42.180
in that snakeskin jacket, they were the closest
to something I'd always wanted, but never

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:44.540
really had: a family"

00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:48.550
So on our 2nd point - nothing is adding up
to deep.

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:49.550
Yet.

00:09:49.550 --> 00:09:52.060
Or we’re a little dense and not getting
it - also an option.

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:56.490
But perhaps all this family stuff leads us
to the ultimate point of Zombieland -- wish

00:09:56.490 --> 00:09:57.950
fulfillment.

00:09:57.950 --> 00:10:03.120
Typically zombie apocalypses are presented
just as it sounds… as an apocalypse.

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:08.190
Society has completely fallen apart post out-break:
People betray and kill one another…

00:10:08.190 --> 00:10:11.160
Nothing is safe and anyone could get eaten
at any moment.

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:15.990
Or they’ll sit around a farm boring us to
death But that’s not the case in Zombieland.

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:21.000
Unlike every other zombie film (where most
of the main characters die or become zombified),

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:22.910
here none of the leads die.

00:10:22.910 --> 00:10:27.320
Even when Tallahassee seems doomed, surrounded
by dozens of zombies, It doesn’t matter.

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:30.820
He ends up killing them all anyway and looking
like a total badass in the process.

00:10:30.820 --> 00:10:35.279
Sure there’s plenty of danger in Zombieland,
plenty of near misses with zombies lunging

00:10:35.279 --> 00:10:37.350
at your face, which isn’t much fun.

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:42.840
But for our virtually indestructive protagonists,
on some level, it’s a perverse kind of paradise.

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:46.450
You can do anything you’ve ever wanted to
with zero consequences.

00:10:46.450 --> 00:10:47.949
Destroy a minivan?

00:10:47.949 --> 00:10:48.949
Check.

00:10:48.949 --> 00:10:51.339
Shoot a firearm in the middle of the street?

00:10:51.339 --> 00:10:52.339
Check!

00:10:52.339 --> 00:10:53.880
Loot any store at any time?

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:54.899
Check again.

00:10:54.899 --> 00:10:58.820
Plus, not only can you live in Bill Murray’s
house, you can meet Bill Murray in person

00:10:58.820 --> 00:11:01.589
AND re-enact scenes from Ghostbusters with
him.

00:11:01.589 --> 00:11:04.240
Until you accitendally kill him

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:08.310
But for the most part, every character gets
everything they want-- Columbus gets to hook

00:11:08.310 --> 00:11:12.940
up with the super hot and smart girl; Tallahassee
gets to kill a whole bunch of zombies; and

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:15.260
they all become their own family.

00:11:15.260 --> 00:11:18.920
The greatest tragedy in the film is that all
the Twinkies get shot to hell.

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:24.640
But even then -- Tallahassee still gets to
chow down on one final remaining one.

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:29.060
So is the point, for Zombieland, that the
apocalypse is a kind of utopia?

00:11:29.060 --> 00:11:32.840
If many people feel like they’re stuck in
a modern hell, Zombieland topples that hell

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:35.180
with a funner, if slightly fraught-with-the-undead
hell.

00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:40.370
One where your whole family’s probably dead,
but hey, you can’t have everything, right?

00:11:40.370 --> 00:11:45.290
This zom-pocalypse though isn’t presented
as something solely to fear, in weird ways,

00:11:45.290 --> 00:11:46.830
it's even a welcome relief.

00:11:46.830 --> 00:11:50.700
You no longer have to deal with who’s polling
well at the Iowa State Fair or worry about

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:52.850
how many likes your vacation photos got.

00:11:52.850 --> 00:11:57.120
"No facebook status updates, y'know, Rob Curtis
is gearin' up for friday"

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:00.709
With almost everyone dead, these concerns
are a thing of the past.

00:12:00.709 --> 00:12:05.000
Now you can reap your just deserts, doing
anything you’ve ever dreamed of.

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:08.740
Killing a few dozen zombies every now and
again doesn’t seem like too steep a cost.

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:13.360
Hell, there’s even a foolproof guide on
how to do so and survive.

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:15.600
And this is kinda smart.

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:17.670
Super deep?

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:18.670
We don’t think so.

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:22.730
But as for the movie as a whole, it’s a
certified Wisecrack maybe-almost-teetering

00:12:22.730 --> 00:12:23.730
on deep.

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:27.160
We have a feeling there was some unrealized
depth with the film’s other themes, but

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:28.870
hey, it’s a fun movie.

00:12:28.870 --> 00:12:29.940
But what do you all think?

00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:32.110
Have we read too much into Zombieland?

00:12:32.110 --> 00:12:34.029
Is it just a fun diversion?

00:12:34.029 --> 00:12:36.310
Or the smartest movie ever made?

00:12:36.310 --> 00:12:38.690
Thanks to all our patrons who support the
channel and our podcasts.

00:12:38.690 --> 00:12:55.100
Don’t forget to hit that subscribe button
and and as always, thanks for watching.

00:12:55.100 --> 00:13:01.420
Peace!

